richard ratcliffe the husband of nazanin  zagar ratcliff is today starting the  19th day  of his hunger strike he's calling on the  government to do more to bring his wife  home  from uh iran  let's talk to the former foreign  secretary now chair of the health and  social committee and that is jeremy hunt  of course mr hunt thanks for joining us  on the programme this morning we chatted  to him richard earlier on in the week  and he was not in good shape when is  this going to be resolved  well i saw him yesterday and first thing  to say is he is an incredibly brave man  and he's brave for two reasons firstly  because he is doing everything possible  to get nasanim released but secondly  because he's the first person in this  situation and there have been many over  the years who's gone public about it and  so the world knows about iran's hostage  taking and i hope one of the things that  comes out of this  is that  iran and other countries that are  thinking of doing this in the future are  shamed into not doing so because it is  an outrageous thing in the 21st century  that countries actually take innocent  people hostage in order to get their way  on diplomatic issues  why when you were foreign secretary did  you not organize for this 400 million  quids worth of debt that we owe the  iranians to be sorted out and then she  could come home  well that is the heart of the issue and  i did make a lot of efforts uh in that  direction i went to tehran  to  meet the then foreign minister to try  and understand what was blocking her  release um and i think we have moved on  now  when i was foreign secretary there was a  debate about the principle of it  and we established that this is not  paying ransom money if it was paying  rats money i wouldn't support paying it  this is paying a debt that an  international court has said we owe  the defense secretary has said we owe  and i think there is now no argument  about the principle of it is difficult  iran is a is a horrible regime that does  the most despicable things so it doesn't  make anyone comfortable doing it but now  it's about the mechanics they are  sanctioned it's not totally easy it is  doable and we've just got to get on and  do it  so we should pay them  we should absolutely pay them because  this is a debt and we are a country that  pays its debts and even if  it is a totally unsavory regime we  should pay this debt there should be no  linkage whatsoever between this and the  case of by the way not just nazanin but  anushia shuri murad tabaz both wonderful  people both innocent both people who  have got incredibly supportive and very  very distressed families and you know  this is definitely an impediment in the  relationship  and whether or not iran sees there to be  a linkage we need to solve this issue  anyway and we should get on and do it  i think there's a meeting today isn't  there with the iranian deputy um fm what  should come from that  well this is actually really good news  it's encouraging uh this deputy foreign  minister is over here because of cop26  and  and i think that  what uh the reason that we're encouraged  about this is that when i was foreign  secretary we always asked ourselves  whether it was a waste of time talking  to the foreign ministry because the  government in iran was really controlled  by the hardliners the revolutionary  guards who reported to the supreme  leader  but now the hardliners have actually  taken over the government so ironically  it might be more productive talking to  the foreign ministry because they might  be speaking to the whole of the  government including the people who are  holding in hostage  um you were also health secretary you've  had lots of titles as part of this  government  and you're now chair of the health and  social care committee where are you  where are you as far as uh double jabbed  um staff uh no longer being able to work  from today in care homes  i think it's a very difficult issue um  the practical issue is you know what is  going to be the impact of some people  leaving as they undoubtedly do but i do  support it because in the end if your  mum or your dad is in a care home or  indeed in a hospital i think you have a  right to know that everyone taking care  of him or her  has taken trouble to make sure they're  not spreading a disease asymptomatically  it is the first responsibility of every  doctor nurse or carer and every single  one that i know has been jabbed twice so  i think it is the right thing to do it  will keep patients safer but i do  recognize there are some practical  challenges  of course you can still pass on kovid  even if you have been double jabbed even  if you've also had your booster is daily  testing not a better option  yes but you know daily testing is not  foolproof either um the one thing you  can do that you know will make a big  difference is to get jabbed and i think  that uh people working in health and  care do understand that and they want to  put the  safety and the protection of the  vulnerable people that they have after  all  given their lives to looking after they  want to put that first so  look it's a really difficult issue it's  difficult in the care sector it's  difficult in the nhs but i do think  sajid javed is right to have bitten the  bullet on it  you're a senior member of the party the  government how embarrassing is it for  our prime minister to be standing up on  the world stage and saying we are not a  corrupt country  well um  you know i'm not a member of the  government but i am a conservative mp  i'm proud to be a conservative mp and  all i would say to  anyone who is laughing at britain uh  laughing at our democracy anywhere in  the world is it is because people like  you are asking people like me these kind  of difficult questions we have one of  the most active  vibrant toughest media scrutineer  politicians anywhere in the world that  we are actually less corrupt than most  countries and that's not to say we're  perfect that's not to say we get  everything right but the fact is we are  a very open robust democracy and i think  ultimately that is actually to our  credit  with that in mind you were there during  the expenses scandal how similar is this  i think it's one of those things where  um  one or two elements of wrongdoing may  cause the rules to be changed um and i  think  there are some gray areas where there  needs to be more clarity  we know that you're not allowed to do  private business from parliamentary  premises  does that apply if someone calls you on  your mobile for example and you happen  to be in your office in parliament and i  think uh  what this will do is mean those kind of  rules are clarified and that's a good  thing  and is it okay to do your business um as  an mp from the british virgin islands  well i don't want to talk about that  individual case k um because that's  going to be investigating choose any  place you want let's say majorca is it  okay to work for several weeks away from  parliament  well there are lots of lovely corners of  the world where you know we would um we  would all love to do our work from but  you know i think in this particular case  we we just need to let the investigation  happen and find out whether the rules  were broken  good to talk to you as always mr hunt  thank you  thank you
 

